A||||7||Gold-86SE||Help?! Broken Head Bolt on Duke||||9||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||12-16-2001||05:49 PM||||<BR>I am pulling the head off an 84, that I knew had at the minimum a blown head gasket.<P>So, I pulled the head off the engine today and one of the bolts is broken directly where the head and the block meet (this bolt is one between the #1 and #2 cylinders).<P>It appears that it has had some time to rust and I am concerned that the bolt is rusted in the block.<P>My primary problem, how do I remove this portion of the head bolt from the block?||||reg||9
Z||000001||avengador1||12-16-2001||06:17 PM||||You could try an easy out, or a reverse cutting drill bit to drill it out, but you would have to retap the threads in the block. You could also just drill it out and put in a helicoil (thread repair kit). You could also try to weld something to it so you can put a wrench to it. Anyway you choose, make sure you soak it over night with a good penetrating oil like pb buster or kroil.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierokid87||12-16-2001||07:11 PM||||Yeah use an easy out.  When my 4 cyld was still in my car it had the same problem.  This is a normal thing for the 2.5 apparently.<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="red"> Fierokid's  Web site:</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="white">http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero</FONT><P><FONT COLOR="blue">AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid</FONT><P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sfiero.JPG">   <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierokid-xmas-sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GTDude||12-16-2001||08:27 PM||||Join the crowd!  Almost all 2.5's of certain years...ones in all vehicles...not just fiero's had this problem....and always the same two bolts.  Is it possible that you have any portion of the bolts still sticking up?  Generally speaking...they are not very tight.  A good set of vice grips should remove them if you have any of the bolt still visible. If not, spray it with pb blaster...absolutely the best penetrant on the market...in my opinion..let it set for 1/2 hour or so.  Use a reverse drill bit and I think you'll be surprised how easily they come out.  Let us know how it turns out.<P>Phil Pettus <P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||10
Z||000004||MrPBody||12-16-2001||09:38 PM||||Been there, done that, and it ain't fun.<P>The exhaust-side center head bolt on my '85 2.5 broke just below the <I>top</I> of the head.  Once I persuaded the head to slide off the broken bolt -- which was not easy -- I unscrewed the head bolt stub with my fingers.  With luck maybe yours will be easy too.  You may be able to back it out with a small chisel.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||12-17-2001||09:25 AM||||<BR>Bad thing is the bolt is broken almost even with the block, and rusted.  So, it looks like fun... not.<P>Anyhow, I keep these suggestions in mind and will post on how it turns out.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gold-86SE||12-18-2001||03:27 PM||||<BR>UPDATE!!!<P>Yesterday, after work I went to Advance Auto purchased an extractor kit sold under the AmPro name.<P>Went home center punched the broken head bolt, took a small drill bit and start a hole in the bolt, using 3-in-1 oil as lube.<P>Next took a bigger bit to the bolt, drilled a hole between 1/8 and 1/4 inch deep, tapped in the extractor, used a medium sized crescent(sp) wrench and turn that dude right out of the threading.<P>An absolutely flawless broken bolt extraction, I am not even worried about tapping the threads because the bolt looked beautiful once out... except that it is broken.<P>All in all, it went way easier than expected.<P>Total time, maybe 15 minutes of actual work.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||avengador1||12-18-2001||06:33 PM||||There you go! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
